  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a sharp upward spike followed by consolidation (e.g., Example 1-4), the model systematically underestimates the subsequent mean-reversion volatility and fails to capture the 'mean reversion' effect where prices oscillate widely around a new higher average.",
    "The model exhibits a strong lag bias in trending markets; it predicts smooth, linear growth but true outcomes display high-frequency spikes and drops that occur within 3-5 days of the forecast horizon start.",
    "Sentiment history (0.20-0.50) acts as a weak leading indicator for volatility expansion rather than direction; when sentiment is low or neutral despite price spikes, the model's linear projection fails to account for the resulting 'overshoot' and subsequent correction.",
    "In bearish regimes (Example 5), the model incorrectly assumes a floor at the last known price, failing to predict deep mean-reversion drops that occur even when sentiment remains near zero or slightly positive.",
    "The forecast horizon is systematically biased toward the immediate post-spike average; true outcomes deviate significantly by +/- 10-15% within the first 7 days due to unmodeled market microstructure noise."
  ]
